## Introduction
Oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) is kineticaly inert, requiring an active and stable catalyst. Although platinum-based catalysts have been regarded as the state-of-the-art catalysts for ORR, the commercial application of Pt-based catalysts are limited by their scarcity and high cost. Therefore, exploring alternative inexpensive electrocatalysts attracts extensive research attention [1]. Carbonbased materials are cheaper and more tunable compared with the Pt-based catalysts. Carbon-based materials have diverse dimensions, including zero-dimensional (oD） fullerene, onedimensional carbon nanotubes (1D CNTs), two-dimensional (2D) graphene, three-dimensional (3D) arbon nanostructures, and their composites. Also, the porous structures, electrical conductivity, and components of carbon-based catalysts are also tunable. Considering the various and adjustable properties of carbon-based materials, it is promising but still challenging to rationally fabricate carbon-based materials electrocatalysts [2-4].
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) formed by different metal ions and organic ligands, have been demonstrated as an important precursor for preparing metal-based heteroatoms-doped porous carbon as catalyst for ORR [5]. Among MOFs, the zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) are a class of porous crystals with extended 3D structures. Zn metal ion in tetrahedral environment is bridged by 2-methylimidazole ligands to form ZIF-8 with a 3D sodalite topology [6]. Most of ZIF-8-derived carbon materials maintain 3D porous structure which inherit from parent precursor [7,8]. Zhang and co-workers revealed that a nitrogendoped carbon (N-C) catalyst derived from the ZIF-8 could exhibit high electrocatalytic performance for ORR in alkaline media [9]. Wang et al. prepared Fe-doped ZIF-8 in an $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -free environment, and then pyrolyzed it into a stable Fe-N-C catalyst. The derived FeN-C catalyst exhibited very high ORR activity in the acidic electrolyte [10].
Compared with porous 3D materials, 2D materials expose more active sites and possess large surface areas, which has attracted enormous research interest. However, MOFs with both 2D crystal structure and 2D morphology are difficult to be utilized as precursor for preparing 2D carbon materials on a large scale, owing to the complicated procedures for synthesis of 2D MOFs and the low yield of 2D MOFs [11,12]. Zn ZIF nanoleaves (ZnZIF-L), which are made up of the same building blocks as ZIF-8, having 2D crystal lattices that stack layer-by-layer into leaf-like morphology [l3]. Also, Zn-ZIF-L can be prepared in an environmental-friendly aqueous solution at room temperature and room pressure. Li et al. obtained cobalt-embedded nitrogendoped mesoporous carbon by directly pyrolyzing $\mathrm{Co/Zn\mathrm{-}}$ containing bimetallic Zn-ZIF-L that prepared by the “shapetransforming method in an aqueous system". The derived cobalt and nitrogen carbon materials retained leaf-like morphology and showed excellent electrocatalytic performance for ORR [14]. Afterwards, Li et al. continued to prepare leaf-like $\mathrm{{Cu/Zn}.}$ containing Zn-ZIF-L to produce the derived cupper and N-C materials,  whichdisplayedconsiderableORRcatalytic performance [15].
The carbon-based electrocatalysts with high degree of graphitization have high electrical conductivity, which is favor of accelerating ORR. But the degree of graphitization of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -ZIF-L derived carbon is limited. CNTs possess high electronic conductivity, which can be used to modify the poor conductivity of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -ZIF-L derived carbon. Generally, there are many different methods to synthesize CNTs, such as chemical vapor deposition [16], plasma enhanced chemical vapor deposition [l7], arc discharge [18] and so on [19]. These methods require relatively harsh conditions. What's more, the entire preparing process consumes high energy. Therefore, a simple method is needed to prepare CNTs-modified Zn-ZIF-L derived carbon nanomaterials.
In this work, we synthesized a hybrid catalyst combined CNTs and $Z\mathrm{n}$ -ZIF-L derived flake-like carbon towards electrocatalysis of ORR by carrying out two-step pyrolysis method. According to previous reports [20-22], dicyanamide is an effective organic substance to produce CNTs via chemical vapor deposition method. Fe has been used as a catalyst to produce carbon nanotubes with adequate carbon and nitrogen sources. And carbon catalysts containing Fe species with Fe-N-C structure display high catalytic activity in the ORR [23-26]. Therefore, we prepared $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -ZIF-L with intact leaf-like morphology via doublesolvent method [27]; then we in situ grew CNTs on $Z\mathrm{n}$ -ZIF-Lderived carbons by carrying out a second pyrolysis method under the catalysis of Fe. The obtained carbon composite shows a novel 1D-on-2D hybrid structure which consists of 1D CNTs and 2D flake-like carbon leaves. The introduction of 1D CNTs with superior electrical conductivity not only is good for the enhancement of electron transportation, but also can boost the dispersion of Fe-based active sites, leading to the facilitation of mass transfer and improvement of utilization efficiency of active sites. Moreover, some Fe-based active sites are embedded within CNTs, which are beneficial to limit aggregation of active sites during electrocatalytic processes for the improvement of stability. Consequently, the hybrid structure combines multiple advantages of hierarchical pores, satisfied degree of graphitization, large specific surface area, and proper nitrogen types; all of which are favorable for ORR.
## 2 Experimental section
## 2.1 Materials and chemicals
All chemicals were obtained from commercial sources and used without further treatment, which are as follows: zinc nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ analytical reagent), potassium hydroxide (KOH, guaranteed reagent), ethanol, hexane, and isopropanol were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co.，Ltd. 2-Methlimidazole (2-mIm, $98\%$ ）was obtained from Shanghai Macklin Biochemical $\mathrm{Co}.$ ，Ltd. Iron (III） chloride hexahydrate $(\mathrm{FeCl_{3}}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H_{2}O},\geq99.0\%)$ was purchased from Shanghai Titan Technology $\mathrm{Co}.$ ，Ltd. Nafion solution $(5\mathrm{~wt.\%})$ and $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ (Platinum, normally $20\%$ on carbon black) were provided by Alfa Aesar.
## 2.2 Preparation of $\mathbf{Fe}_{7\%}$ -ZIF-L nanoleaves
Aqueous solution of $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O}$ 0 $\mathrm{0.28~g,20~mL}$ ）wasadded into aqueous solution of 2-mIm $(1.08\mathrm{g},20\mathrm{mL})$ ）and the mixture was vigorously stirred for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ at room temperature. The white product was washed with deionized water, separated by centrifugation, and dried at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ 、 This white product was assigned as $Z\mathrm{n}$ -ZIF-L.
$Z\mathrm{n}$ -ZIF-L $(0.2\ \mathrm{g)}$ was well-dispersed in ethanol $\mathrm{\langle}5\mathrm{\mL\rangle}$ ）under sonication for $5~\mathrm{min}.$ and hexane（ $\mathrm{10~mL})$ ）was poured into the ethanol solution. After stirring for $20\mathrm{min}$ 。 $676\upmu\mathrm{L}$ ethanol solution of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ 1 $(20\mathrm{\mg{mL^{-1}}},$ ）was injected into the above mixture.After stirring for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ at room temperature, the resulting sample was collected by centrifugation, and dried at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ Therefore, in this work, the prepared Fe-containing $Z\mathrm{n}$ -ZIF-L precursor with light yellow was named as $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}–\mathrm{ZIF-L}$ (note: The yield of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -ZIF-L derived carbon was about $20\%$ thus the mass concentration of Fe in the final carbon composited was around $7\%$ 。
## 2.3  Preparation of $\bf{F e}_{7\%}–\bf{N}/C$
$\mathrm{Fe_{7\%}}\mathrm{-N/C}$ was obtained by directly carbonizing $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -ZIF-L at $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under a flow of $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ for $2\mathrm{h}$ with a heating rate of $5^{\circ}\mathrm{C}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$
## 2.4 Preparation of $\mathbf{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L-CNT- $x$
First, the as-prepared $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}–\mathrm{N}/\mathrm{C}$ and dicyanamide (mass ratio $\c=$ 1:20) were placed in a corundum boat (dicyanamide was located at the upstream side) and were put in a tube furnace under protection by $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ atmosphere. The heating program run at a rate of $10\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ and kept at $900~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Then, resulting black powders were preleached in 0.5 M $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $8\ \mathrm{h}.$ In the final step, the catalyst that obtained through second-pyrolysis was labeled as $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L-CNT-900.
## 2.5 Characterization
All of the X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were measured on a Rigaku Ultima IV diffractometer by using Cu $\mathrm{K}\alpha$ radiation $(35~\mathrm{kV},25~\mathrm{mA}$ ）at room temperature. The morphologies of the samples were characterized with scanning electron microscopy (SEM, Hitachi S4800） at an accelerating voltage of $10~\mathrm{\kV}$ Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images were obtained on a FEI Tecnai G2 F30 microscope at an accelerating voltage of $300\mathrm{kV}$ X-ray elemental spectroscopy (EDS) and high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) images were characterized from LVEMS JEM2100F microscope operated at an accelerating voltage of $200\ \mathrm{kV}.$ Raman spectra were obtained at room temperature on an inVia Reflex (Renishaw). $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption analysis  was measured on a Quantachrome Autosorb-iQ instrument at $77~\mathrm{K}.$ Specific surface areas and pore size distributions were calculated based on the multi-point Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) method and density functional theory (DFT), the micro surface areas were based on t-plot method. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analyses were carried out on a Kratos AXIS Ultra spectrometer employing Al $\mathrm{K}\alpha$ radiation, with a spot size of $400\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . All the binding energies were calibrated via referencing to C ls binding energy $(284.7\mathrm{eV})$ ”
## 2.6 Electrochemical measurements
Electrochemical tests were conducted on a CHI 760E electrochemical analyzer (Shanghai Chen Hua Ltd., China) coupled with a rotating ring disk electrode (RRDE, ALS Co., Ltd). All electrochemical measurements were carried out in a standard three-electrode setup by using a Pt wire and saturated calomel electrode (SCE) as the counter and reference electrodes, respectively. Catalyst inks of $2\ \mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ of $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}–\mathrm{L}–\mathrm{CNT}–x$ were prepared by dispersing the respective black powders in a $5\mathrm{\wt.\%}$ solution of nafion in isopropanol and ultra-pure water with ultrasonication. $12.5~{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ catalyst inks were then dropped onto freshly polished glassy carbon electrode ( $\mathrm{~\dot{~}4~m m}$ in diameter) as working electrodes with thin-film catalyst loadings of $0.20\mathrm{\mg{z}}\mathrm{{cm}}^{-2}$ ：All the measured potentials (vs. SCE reference electrode) in this work were converted to the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) scale based on Eq. (1)
$$
E\left(\mathrm{vs.RHE}\right)=E\left(\mathrm{vs.SCE}\right)+0.241+0.0591\times\mathrm{pH}
$$
$0.1\mathrm{~M~KOH}$ electrolyte saturated with $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ bubbling for at least $30\mathrm{min}$ Cyclic voltammetry (CV) experiments were carried out at a rate of $50\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1};$ and the potential range $0.0{-}1.2\mathrm{V}$ was scanned (note: onset potential, when polarized current density achieves $0.10\mathrm{\mA{\cdot}c m^{-2}}$ . Liner sweep voltammetry (LSV) rate was tested with a scan $5~\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}s^{-1}$ at room temperature, with rotation speeds ranging from 400 to $2,025~\mathrm{rpm}$ . The ring electrode potential was set to $\mathrm{v}$ versus RHE.
RRDE tests were performed on the CHI 760E workstation, with an electrolyte of $0.1\mathrm{~M~KOH}.$ 、a disk electrode sweeping rate of $5\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ a ring electrode potential of $1.4\mathrm{V}_{\cdot}$ The electron-transfer number $(n)$ and the hydrogen peroxide yield $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\%)$ from the ORR process were calculated from Eqs. (2) and (3)
$$
n=4\times\frac{I_{\mathrm{d}}}{I_{\mathrm{d}}+I_{\mathrm{r}}/N}
$$
$$
\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\%=200\times\frac{I_{\mathrm{r}}/N}{I_{\mathrm{d}}+I_{\mathrm{r}}/N}
$$
where $I_{\mathrm{d}}$ and $I_{\mathrm{r}}$ represent the disk current and ring current, respectively, and $N$ means the current collection efficiency (0.4) of the Pt ring.
The kinetics current was calculated using the Eq. (4)
