I_{\mathrm{K}}={\frac{I\times I_{\mathrm{L}}}{I_{\mathrm{L}}-I}}
$$
where $I_{\mathrm{K}}$ is the kinetic current, $I_{\mathrm{L}}$ refers to the measured diffusionlimited current, and $I$ is the experimentally obtained current.
$$
I_{\mathrm{K,specific}}\big(\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{-2}\big)=\frac{I_{\mathrm{K}}}{m\times\mathrm{SSA}}
$$
The mass  activity current $\mathrm{\Delta}^{\prime}I_{\mathrm{K}},$ mass, $\mathbf{A{\cdotg}^{-1}}.$ ）normalized against the catalyst-loading and specific activity current $(I_{\mathrm{K}},$ specific, $\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{-2}.$ ) normalized on the specific surface area (SSA) of asprepared catalyst were obtained by means of the Eqs. (5) and (6), respectively
$$
I_{\mathrm{K,mass}}(\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1})={\frac{I_{\mathrm{K}}}{m}}
$$
where $I_{\mathrm{K}}$ is the ORR kinetic current (mA） at $0.85\mathrm{V},m$ is the asprepared catalyst loading on the RRDE $(m=0.20~\mathrm{mg})$ ）andthe SSA is the specific surface area $(\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ obtained by $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherm.
