As shown by SEM and TEM images in Fig.l,the obtained representative $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L-CNT-900 sample retained the original flakelike shape and consisted of a lot of CNTs. There are some nanoparticles in $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L-CNT-900. In addition, as the temperature increases, there are much more CNTs existed on derived carbon materials (Fig.S3 in the ESM). After carefully observed by the HRTEM image (Fig.l(d)), the interlayer spacing of the nanoparticles was $0.203\ \mathrm{nm},$ consistent with the (220） facet of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}$ phase $(\mathrm{PDF}\#35–0772)$ . The corresponding elemental mapping images in Figs. 2(b)-2(d) show the homogeneous distribution of $\mathrm{C},\mathrm{N},$ and Fe. In order to understand the state of carbon and Fe in $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}\mathrm{-L}\mathrm{-CNT}{-x}$ 0 $\dot{\boldsymbol{x}}=800\mathrm{;}$ 、900, and 1,000), XRD patterns were performed and shown in Fig. 3(a). All the samples show the distinct characteristic peaks at $26^{\circ}$ which can be assigned to the (0o2) diffraction of graphite carbon and become more shape at higher temperatures. Diffraction peaks located at $44.6^{\circ}:$ which corresponds to the (220) plane of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}$ (PDF#35-0772), are shown more obviously in $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L-CNT-900 and $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L-CNT-1000 than $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L-CNT-800, implying that the higher temperature is in favor of forming $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}$ nanoparticles. These features are coincident with HRTEM data (Fig.1(d)). $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}$ nanoparticles encapsulated in carbon materials have been regarded as effective active sites for catalyzing ORR [35-37]. Additional diffraction peaks which can be attributed to $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ were also found in $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L-CNT-900 and $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L-CNT-1000. The iron oxide might be formed from the aggregation of Fe species under higher temperatures and oxidation by air 