## 3.2 ORR performance investigation
The electrochemical ORR catalytic activities of $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L-CNT- $x$ 0 $\mathbf{\chi}_{x}=\mathbf{\chi}_{\mathbf{\chi}}$ 800, 900, and 1,o00） were characterized by using cyclic voltammograms(CVs） in $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -saturated $0.1\mathrm{~M~KOH}$ as shown in the Fig. 4(a). $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}.$ -L-CNT- $x$ behaved the ORR catalytic activity in a potential range of $0.8\mathrm{-}1.0\mathrm{V}$ in alkaline electrolyte. As shown in the LSV in Fig.4(b), $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L-CNT-900 exhibits an excellent ORR activity with an onset potential $(E_{\mathrm{onset}})$ of $1.040\mathrm{V}$ and a half-wave potential $(E_{1/2})$ of $0.88~\mathrm{V}_{:}$ which exceeds that of commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ 1 $\begin{array}{r}{'{E}_{\mathrm{onset}}=1.000\:\mathrm{V}.}\end{array}$ $E_{1/2}=0.85\:\mathrm{V}.$ $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L-CNT-800 $\begin{array}{r}{^{\prime}E_{\mathrm{onset}}=0.966\mathrm{V},}\end{array}$ $E_{1/2}=0.73\mathrm{~V},$ ，and $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L-CNT-1000 $\mathit{\Delta}^{\prime}E_{\mathrm{onset}}=0.965\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ $E_{1/2}=$ $0.84\mathrm{V}$ . Furthermore, the specific activity normalized to the mass loading and specific activity normalized to the specific surface area are used to evaluate the catalytic performance of resulting materials. $\mathrm{Fe_{7\%}\mathrm{-}L\mathrm{-}C N T\mathrm{-}900}$ has the largest ORR mass activity $(\sim3.22~\mathrm{A{\cdotg}^{-1})}$ than $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L-CNT-800 $(\sim2.41~\mathrm{A{\cdotg}^{-1})}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L
CNT-1000 $(\sim~2.93~\mathrm{{\A{g}^{-1}}})$ . In addition, $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L-CNT-900 also exhibits superior ORR specific activity $\left(\sim6,288.58~\mathrm{A{\cdot}m^{-2}}\right)$ than $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L-CNT-800 $(\sim~5,089.37~\mathrm{\A{\cdot}m^{-2}})$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L-CNT-1000 $\left(\sim5,838.03\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{-2}\right)$
Based on the above investigation, we found that $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L-CNT900 performed the best performance for ORR. Besides the calcination temperature, the concentrations of Fe in Fe-ZIF-L precursor also play a significant role in determining the properties of the derived hybrid carbon materials as well as  their electrochemical performance. Hence, we prepared a series of FeZIF-L with different concentration of Fe ( $0\%$ $1\%.$ and $15\%$ ）and pyrolyzed them at the optimized $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to produce more hybrid carbon-based catalysts, assigned as NC-900, $\mathrm{Fe}_{1\%}$ -L-CNT-900, and $\mathrm{Fe}_{15\%}$ -L-CNT-9o0. The morphology and structures can be seen in Figs. S1(b), S5, and S6 in the ESM, respectively. $\mathrm{Fe}_{1\%}$ -L-CNT-900 and $\mathrm{Fe}_{15\%}$ -L-CNT-900 are the same as $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L-CNT-900. NC-900 lost the morphology of parent leaves, which was consistent with the previous report [51]. According to the LSV test (Fig. S7 in the ESM),NC-900 has the worst electrocatalytic activity with $E_{\mathrm{onset}}$ of $0.807~\mathrm{V}$ and $E_{1/2}$ of $0.66\mathrm{~V~}$ . After introducing Fe in the catalyst, $\mathrm{Fe}_{1\%}$ -L-CNT-900 exhibits better catalytic performance than NC900 $\mathrm{^{\prime}}E_{\mathrm{onset}}=0.986\mathrm{~V}.$ $E_{1/2}=0.81\mathrm{~V};$ , proving the importance of introducing Fe in $Z\mathrm{n}$ -ZIF-L precursors. Properly increasing the concentration of Fe is beneficial to introduce more active sites and increase the degree of graphitization, which improves the catalytic activity of resulting materials. But simply increasing the dosage of Fe in precursors is not effective. When the content of Fe increased to some extent, the excessive Fe will aggregate into large Fe nanoparticles. Nanoporous carbon will be catalytically graphitized by large Fe nanoparticles, leading to a higher degree of graphitization but much lower doping of nitrogen. The electrocatalytic performance of $\mathrm{Fe_{15\%}\mathrm{-L\mathrm{-CNT-900}}}$ 1 $\mathrm{'}E_{\mathrm{onset}}=0.961\mathrm{~V}_{\mathrm{:}}$ $E_{1/2}=0.84\mathrm{~V})$ is lower than $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L-CNT-900. This result is in agreement with the previous report that excessive Fe will aggregate to form inactive iron compounds so that decreases the catalytic ability of derived materials [52]. $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L-CNT-900 shows the greatest electrocatalytic activity, probably due to the optimized balance of active Fe species, ratio of graphitic N and pyridinic N, degree of graphitization, hierarchically micro- and mesoporous structures and specific surface area.
Electrocatalytic activity of $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L-CNT-900 for ORR was studied in detail by investigating the polarization curves at various rotating rates from 400 to $2{,}025~\mathrm{rpm}$ as indicated in Fig. 4(c) and the corresponding Koutecky-Levich plot was shown in Fig. S8 in the ESM.Besides, $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L-CNT-800, $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L-CNT-900, and $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -LCNT-1000 gave Tafel slopes of 316.5, 109.0, and $119.8~\mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}}$ 。 respectively (Fig.4(d)), demonstrating that $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L-CNT-900 exhibits fast ORR kinetics. To further investigate the ORR pathways,we carried out RRDE measurements to monitor the formation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ during the ORR process (shown in Fig.S9 in the ESM). The electron-transfer number $(n)$ value for ORR was calculated as shown in Fig.5(a). The number is about 3.99, which shows that a four-electron mechanism for ORR prevails over a two-electron mechanism in this work. Moreover, as shown in Fig. 5(a), the yield of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ produced on the $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L-CNT-900 during the ORR process is less than $2.24\%$ in 0.l M KOH solution, indicating high reaction efficiency. In order to further assess its stability, successive scanning CVs in $\mathrm{~O}_{2}$ -saturated $0.1\mathrm{~M~KOH}$ solution were recorded on $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L-CNT-900 catalyst. As displayed inFig.5(b),after3,oooCVcycles,thecatalystshowedanegativeshiftc $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{f}E_{1/2}\mathrm{=}$ $20~\mathrm{\mV}$ . The results show that the $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L-CNT-900 catalyst exhibits a relatively stable electrocatalytic activity compared with commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalyst. Furthermore, the morphology of $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -LCNT-900 after the durability test was examined by SEM and TEM (Fig. Sl0 in the ESM). It is clearly seen that there were no noticeable changes after the long-time stability test. As summarized in Table S4 in the ESM, the performance of our catalysts are comparable to the previous reported non-precious metal/carbon composites.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, we have successfully prepared catalysts consisting of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}$ nanoparticles, 1D CNTs, and flake-like carbon leaves. The calcination temperature and Fe concentration play crucial roles in formation of resulting catalysts consisting of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}$ and CNTs. The optimal hybrid electrocatalyst of $\mathrm{Fe}_{7\%}$ -L-CNT-900，with active $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C},$ moderate ratios of pyridinic $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ and graphitic N, hierarchically micro- and mesoporous structures and largest specific surface area $(512.4~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ，displays an outstanding ORR activity than the commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalyst.Moreover, this work can inspire a new thought for the synthesis of non-precious-metal catalyst with a hybrid structure for ORR.
